WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.779
To end the lecture

00:00:01.857 --> 00:00:04.270
here are three classic problems

00:00:04.348 --> 00:00:06.372
about binary search trees.

00:00:06.372 --> 00:00:07.540
The first one

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:10.420
does the order of operations matter?

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.869
Two operations commute if doing them in

00:00:13.948 --> 00:00:16.729
either order gives the same result.

00:00:16.729 --> 00:00:19.272
Take this tree, with root 4

00:00:19.272 --> 00:00:22.530
and delete 5 and 6, in both orders.

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:25.349
On the left, 5 first.

00:00:25.349 --> 00:00:29.360
5 is a leaf, so we just cut it off.

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:32.018
Now 6 has one child, 8.

00:00:32.018 --> 00:00:33.291
Splice 6 out

00:00:33.291 --> 00:00:35.997
4's right child becomes 8

00:00:35.997 --> 00:00:38.225
and 7 comes up with it.

00:00:38.225 --> 00:00:39.817
The result is 4

00:00:39.817 --> 00:00:42.125
with children 2 and 8

00:00:42.125 --> 00:00:44.910
and 7 below 8 on the left.

00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:47.653
On the right, 6 first.

00:00:47.653 --> 00:00:51.707
6 has two children, so this is case 3.

00:00:51.797 --> 00:00:54.320
Its successor is the minimum

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:57.204
of the subtree at 8: 7.

00:00:57.204 --> 00:00:59.727
Copy 7 into 6's node

00:00:59.727 --> 00:01:02.610
and cut off the old leaf 7.

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:06.017
Then 5, which is still a leaf.

00:01:06.017 --> 00:01:06.758
Cut it.

00:01:06.758 --> 00:01:08.611
The result is 4

00:01:08.611 --> 00:01:11.297
with children 2 and 7

00:01:11.297 --> 00:01:15.280
and 8 is now the right child of 7.

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:18.881
Delete 5, then 6, gives one shape

00:01:18.881 --> 00:01:21.205
8 on top, 7 below it.

00:01:21.205 --> 00:01:24.149
Delete 6, then 5, gives another

00:01:24.149 --> 00:01:26.473
7 on top, 8 below it.

00:01:26.473 --> 00:01:28.796
So deletion does not commute

00:01:28.796 --> 00:01:31.740
as far as the shape of the tree goes.

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:35.324
But both trees hold the same set of keys

00:01:35.324 --> 00:01:38.557
their in-order walks are both 2, 4

00:01:38.557 --> 00:01:39.662
7, 8.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:42.974
A program that only asks whether a key is

00:01:43.053 --> 00:01:46.050
in the set cannot tell the difference.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:48.267
The shapes still matter.

00:01:48.267 --> 00:01:49.822
Heights can differ

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:52.078
and so can the cost of every

00:01:52.156 --> 00:01:53.400
later operation.

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:55.924
Insertion is no better.

00:01:55.924 --> 00:01:59.643
Start from 4, with children 2 and 6.

00:01:59.643 --> 00:02:00.827
Insert 7

00:02:00.827 --> 00:02:03.785
it becomes the right child of 6.

00:02:03.785 --> 00:02:07.420
Then 8 goes below 7, on the right.

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:09.213
In the other order

00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:11.493
8 comes first and becomes

00:02:11.568 --> 00:02:12.936
6's right child.

00:02:13.012 --> 00:02:15.443
Then 7 walks down past 8

00:02:15.519 --> 00:02:17.570
and becomes its left child.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:20.910
The program prints both: the same keys

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:22.520
two different trees.

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:25.074
Insertions of keys that end up in

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:27.010
different places do commute.

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:29.748
Insert 1 and 5 into the small tree

00:02:29.748 --> 00:02:31.952
they hang below different nodes

00:02:31.952 --> 00:02:34.422
so either order gives the same tree.

00:02:34.489 --> 00:02:36.760
It is when one new key lands below

00:02:36.826 --> 00:02:39.030
the other that the order matters.

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:41.715
The second problem

00:02:41.715 --> 00:02:45.592
sorting with a binary search tree.

00:02:45.592 --> 00:02:50.578
To sort an array, insert its elements one by

00:02:50.689 --> 00:02:53.237
one into an empty tree.

00:02:53.237 --> 00:02:57.447
15, 6, 18, 3, 7

00:02:57.447 --> 00:03:00.770
17, 20, 2, 4.

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:04.657
Then walk the tree in-order and write

00:03:04.753 --> 00:03:07.526
the keys back into the array.

00:03:07.526 --> 00:03:11.350
Out they come in increasing order: 2

00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:15.652
3, 4, 6, 7, 15, 17

00:03:15.652 --> 00:03:17.373
18, 20.

00:03:17.373 --> 00:03:20.146
That is the whole algorithm

00:03:20.146 --> 00:03:22.440
often called tree sort.

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:26.366
In Java, sort starts with an empty root.

00:03:26.366 --> 00:03:27.955
For every value v

00:03:27.955 --> 00:03:30.882
root becomes insert(root, v)

00:03:30.882 --> 00:03:33.391
the same insertion as before

00:03:33.391 --> 00:03:36.402
with a counter for the comparisons.

00:03:36.402 --> 00:03:38.242
Then fill(root, a, 0)

00:03:38.242 --> 00:03:40.500
writes the keys back.

00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:43.876
fill is an in-order walk that carries

00:03:43.958 --> 00:03:45.757
the next free index i.

00:03:45.757 --> 00:03:48.375
It fills from the left subtree

00:03:48.375 --> 00:03:51.810
puts x.key at index i and moves i on

00:03:51.892 --> 00:03:54.591
then fills from the right subtree

00:03:54.672 --> 00:03:57.290
and returns the next free index.

00:03:57.290 --> 00:04:00.970
The walk visits every node once: Θ(n).

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:04.937
The program also sorts 2000 keys

00:04:04.937 --> 00:04:07.581
once already sorted, 0, 1

00:04:07.581 --> 00:04:10.473
2 and so on, and once scrambled

00:04:10.556 --> 00:04:13.282
by multiplying i by a large prime

00:04:13.365 --> 00:04:15.430
and taking the remainder.

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:19.299
Predict: how many comparisons does the sort

00:04:19.381 --> 00:04:22.000
method make on the sorted input?

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:25.519
And roughly how many on the scrambled one?

00:04:25.519 --> 00:04:28.220
Pause the video and work it out.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:35.392
On sorted input, every key is larger than

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:37.495
all the keys before it

00:04:37.495 --> 00:04:40.824
so each one goes right, right, right

00:04:40.824 --> 00:04:42.839
to the end of a chain.

00:04:42.927 --> 00:04:45.731
The key at position i walks past

00:04:45.818 --> 00:04:48.359
all i − 1 earlier keys.

00:04:48.359 --> 00:04:51.338
Adding up 0 + 1 + 2

00:04:51.338 --> 00:04:53.878
and so on up to n − 1

00:04:53.878 --> 00:04:57.120
gives n(n − 1)/2.

00:04:57.470 --> 00:05:00.786
For 2000 keys that is

00:05:00.865 --> 00:05:02.759
1,999,000

00:05:02.838 --> 00:05:04.496
comparisons.

00:05:04.575 --> 00:05:06.943
The scrambled input needs only

00:05:07.022 --> 00:05:10.417
38,920

00:05:10.417 --> 00:05:12.390
about 50 times fewer

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:14.759
because the tree stays bushy.

00:05:14.759 --> 00:05:16.495
Same keys, same code

00:05:16.495 --> 00:05:18.390
only the order differs.

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:22.221
In general, the in-order walk is Θ(n)

00:05:22.295 --> 00:05:25.103
and the insertions cost the sum of the

00:05:25.177 --> 00:05:27.394
depths at which the keys land.

00:05:27.394 --> 00:05:29.463
If the tree stays balanced

00:05:29.463 --> 00:05:31.828
sorting takes Θ(n log n)

00:05:31.901 --> 00:05:33.749
and so it does on average

00:05:33.823 --> 00:05:35.227
over random orders.

00:05:35.227 --> 00:05:38.330
On sorted input it is Θ(n²).

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:40.025
Here is a nice fact.

00:05:40.093 --> 00:05:43.052
Tree sort makes exactly the same comparisons

00:05:43.119 --> 00:05:45.809
as quicksort that always picks the first

00:05:45.877 --> 00:05:47.289
element as its pivot.

00:05:47.289 --> 00:05:49.710
The root, 15, plays the pivot

00:05:49.778 --> 00:05:51.930
every later key is compared with

00:05:51.997 --> 00:05:53.611
it and goes to one side

00:05:53.611 --> 00:05:55.158
the smaller keys left

00:05:55.158 --> 00:05:56.705
the larger keys right

00:05:56.705 --> 00:05:58.454
exactly like a partition.

00:05:58.454 --> 00:06:01.077
Then each side is sorted the same way

00:06:01.077 --> 00:06:03.901
with 6 and 18 as the next pivots.

00:06:03.969 --> 00:06:06.726
So tree sort has the same average and the

00:06:06.793 --> 00:06:09.080
same worst case as that quicksort.

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:11.959
In practice tree sort is slower

00:06:12.033 --> 00:06:14.183
than quicksort or merge sort.

00:06:14.183 --> 00:06:17.297
It allocates a Node object for every key

00:06:17.297 --> 00:06:19.818
uses about four times the memory

00:06:19.892 --> 00:06:22.561
and follows pointers all over memory

00:06:22.635 --> 00:06:24.785
instead of scanning an array.

00:06:24.785 --> 00:06:26.046
A balanced tree

00:06:26.046 --> 00:06:28.492
in Lectures 15 and 16

00:06:28.492 --> 00:06:30.420
would fix the worst case.

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:33.587
But the real strength of a binary

00:06:33.673 --> 00:06:36.404
search tree is not sorting once.

00:06:36.404 --> 00:06:40.160
It is keeping data sorted while it changes

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:41.526
insert, delete

00:06:41.526 --> 00:06:44.770
and ask for the next key at any time.

00:06:45.350 --> 00:06:46.777
The third problem

00:06:46.777 --> 00:06:50.186
the lowest common ancestor, LCA for short

00:06:50.186 --> 00:06:51.296
of two nodes.

00:06:51.375 --> 00:06:53.832
It is the deepest node that has

00:06:53.912 --> 00:06:56.131
both of them as descendants

00:06:56.211 --> 00:06:58.113
where a node counts as a

00:06:58.192 --> 00:06:59.857
descendant of itself.

00:06:59.857 --> 00:07:01.839
For 5 and 21

00:07:01.839 --> 00:07:04.772
both 25 and 20 qualify

00:07:04.772 --> 00:07:07.150
and 20 is the deeper one.

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:10.284
In a BST the keys tell us where to go.

00:07:10.284 --> 00:07:11.676
Start at the root.

00:07:11.749 --> 00:07:13.507
If both keys are smaller

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:15.119
than the current key

00:07:15.119 --> 00:07:17.756
both nodes are in the left subtree

00:07:17.756 --> 00:07:19.953
and so is their LCA: go left.

00:07:19.953 --> 00:07:22.517
If both keys are larger, go right.

00:07:22.591 --> 00:07:25.155
Otherwise the keys are on different

00:07:25.228 --> 00:07:27.132
sides of the current node

00:07:27.132 --> 00:07:29.770
or one of them is the current node.

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:32.260
Then the current node is the LCA.

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:36.240
In Java it is one loop down the tree.

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:40.008
If a and b are both less than x.key

00:07:40.008 --> 00:07:42.122
x moves to x.left.

00:07:42.122 --> 00:07:45.522
If both are greater, to x.right.

00:07:45.522 --> 00:07:47.360
Otherwise return x.

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:50.217
The loop only ends without an

00:07:50.304 --> 00:07:52.724
answer if the tree runs out

00:07:52.724 --> 00:07:54.971
and then it returns null.

00:07:54.971 --> 00:07:57.651
Each step goes one level down

00:07:57.651 --> 00:08:00.590
so the walk is one path: O(h).

00:08:01.170 --> 00:08:03.678
The demo asks for more pairs.

00:08:03.678 --> 00:08:05.062
10 and 30

00:08:05.148 --> 00:08:07.829
10 is smaller than 25

00:08:07.915 --> 00:08:09.731
and 30 is larger

00:08:09.731 --> 00:08:12.066
so they split at the root.

00:08:12.066 --> 00:08:14.142
The LCA is 25.

00:08:14.228 --> 00:08:16.390
5 and 32 split

00:08:16.477 --> 00:08:19.330
at the root as well: 25.

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:22.052
10 and 23 are both

00:08:22.134 --> 00:08:24.915
smaller than 25: go left.

00:08:24.915 --> 00:08:25.815
At 20

00:08:25.815 --> 00:08:29.332
10 is smaller and 23 is larger

00:08:29.332 --> 00:08:30.887
the LCA is 20.

00:08:30.887 --> 00:08:32.359
And 10 and 5

00:08:32.359 --> 00:08:35.958
at 20 both are smaller, go left to 10.

00:08:36.040 --> 00:08:38.167
Now one of the keys is the

00:08:38.248 --> 00:08:41.684
current node itself, so the answer is 10.

00:08:41.684 --> 00:08:44.138
A node is its own descendant.

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:46.592
Every one of these walks is a

00:08:46.674 --> 00:08:48.800
single path from the root.

00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:51.349
One more tree: 15

00:08:51.349 --> 00:08:53.918
with children 10 and 20

00:08:53.918 --> 00:08:57.514
where 10 has children 5 and 13

00:08:57.599 --> 00:08:59.740
and 13 has children

00:08:59.825 --> 00:09:01.538
12 and 14.

00:09:01.538 --> 00:09:03.764
LCA(5, 14).

00:09:03.764 --> 00:09:07.531
At 15, both keys are smaller: go left.

00:09:07.531 --> 00:09:08.216
At 10

00:09:08.216 --> 00:09:11.640
5 is smaller and 14 is larger.

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:15.065
The keys split here, so the LCA is 10

00:09:15.151 --> 00:09:17.462
even though 14 is two

00:09:17.548 --> 00:09:19.260
levels further down.

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:20.347
A warning.

00:09:20.421 --> 00:09:22.484
The method assumes that both

00:09:22.558 --> 00:09:24.106
keys are in the tree.

00:09:24.106 --> 00:09:27.348
If one is missing, it still returns a node

00:09:27.348 --> 00:09:30.665
the place where the two searches would part.

00:09:30.665 --> 00:09:33.170
To be safe, find both keys first.

00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:36.731
Here is the whole lecture in one table.

00:09:36.731 --> 00:09:39.635
find goes left if the key is smaller

00:09:39.635 --> 00:09:41.392
right if it is larger.

00:09:41.469 --> 00:09:44.220
findMin and findMax follow

00:09:44.296 --> 00:09:46.895
left or right pointers to the end.

00:09:46.971 --> 00:09:49.111
The successor is the minimum

00:09:49.187 --> 00:09:50.792
of the right subtree

00:09:50.869 --> 00:09:53.161
or else we climb until we come

00:09:53.238 --> 00:09:54.843
up from a left child.

00:09:54.843 --> 00:09:57.670
The predecessor is the mirror image.

00:09:58.020 --> 00:10:00.344
insert searches with a trailing

00:10:00.419 --> 00:10:02.594
pointer and hangs a new leaf.

00:10:02.594 --> 00:10:04.093
delete cuts a leaf

00:10:04.093 --> 00:10:06.717
splices out a node with one child

00:10:06.792 --> 00:10:10.016
and for two children copies the successor's

00:10:10.091 --> 00:10:12.340
key and deletes the successor.

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:15.785
Every one of them walks one path

00:10:15.785 --> 00:10:18.410
so every one costs O(h).

00:10:18.990 --> 00:10:19.844
To sum up.

00:10:19.844 --> 00:10:20.270
One

00:10:20.270 --> 00:10:23.768
insertions and deletions do not commute.

00:10:23.768 --> 00:10:27.181
Both orders leave the same set of keys

00:10:27.181 --> 00:10:30.594
but the shapes, and so the later costs

00:10:30.594 --> 00:10:31.618
can differ.

00:10:31.618 --> 00:10:34.434
Two: tree sort inserts every key

00:10:34.519 --> 00:10:36.140
and walks in-order.

00:10:36.226 --> 00:10:39.639
It costs Θ(n log n) on average and

00:10:39.724 --> 00:10:42.710
Θ(n²) on sorted input.

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:46.962
Three: the lowest common ancestor in a BST

00:10:47.055 --> 00:10:49.935
is where the two searches part

00:10:49.935 --> 00:10:53.465
found on one path down from the root

00:10:53.465 --> 00:10:54.580
in O(h).

00:10:54.930 --> 00:10:55.698
Try this.

00:10:55.698 --> 00:10:58.000
Write floor(root, k)

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:00.985
the largest key that is at most k

00:11:00.985 --> 00:11:03.970
using one walk down from the root.

00:11:03.970 --> 00:11:06.955
In our running tree from part two

00:11:06.955 --> 00:11:09.258
what is floor(14)?

00:11:09.258 --> 00:11:13.010
Think about when a key becomes a candidate.

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:15.103
Next lecture: AVL trees

00:11:15.103 --> 00:11:18.079
which repair their shape with rotations

00:11:18.079 --> 00:11:20.837
so that the height stays O(log n)

00:11:20.837 --> 00:11:23.160
whatever the order of the keys.

